{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c96bae59-d673-48ee-91cd-eddc60dca2f6","step":{"id":"s1","script":"These triangles have the same shape but different sizes. Matching angles tell us which vertices, and therefore which sides, correspond.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"C1","content":"Similar triangles"},{"op":"figure","id":"triangles","region":"left","frame":{"x":[-1,12],"y":[-1,9],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"a","at":[0,0],"label":"A"},{"kind":"point","id":"b","at":[3,0],"label":"B"},{"kind":"point","id":"c","at":[0,4],"label":"C"},{"kind":"polyline","id":"small","points":[{"at":"a"},{"at":"b"},{"at":"c"}],"closed":true},{"kind":"point","id":"d","at":[5,0],"label":"D"},{"kind":"point","id":"e","at":[11,0],"label":"E"},{"kind":"point","id":"f","at":[5,8],"label":"F"},{"kind":"polyline","id":"large","points":[{"at":"d"},{"at":"e"},{"at":"f"}],"closed":true},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"a"},"from":{"at":"b"},"to":{"at":"c"}},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"d"},"from":{"at":"e"},"to":{"at":"f"}},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"b"},"from":{"at":"c"},"to":{"at":"a"},"label":"θ"},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"e"},"from":{"at":"f"},"to":{"at":"d"},"label":"θ"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"similar","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\triangle ABC\\sim\\triangle DEF"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c96bae59-d673-48ee-91cd-eddc60dca2f6","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Compare matching sides in the same order: large divided by small. Both the bases and heights give a scale factor of two.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"lengths","on":"triangles","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":{"at":"a"},"to":{"at":"b"},"label":"3"},{"kind":"measure","from":{"at":"d"},"to":{"at":"e"},"label":"6"},{"kind":"measure","from":{"at":"a"},"to":{"at":"c"},"label":"4"},{"kind":"measure","from":{"at":"d"},"to":{"at":"f"},"label":"8"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"scale","anchor":{"el":"similar","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\frac{DE}{AB}=\\frac{DF}{AC}=\\frac{6}{3}=\\frac{8}{4}=2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"c96bae59-d673-48ee-91cd-eddc60dca2f6","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The same scale factor applies to every corresponding side. So a hypotenuse of five becomes ten. Consistent ratios are what proportional sides means.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"hypotenuses","on":"triangles","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":{"at":"b"},"to":{"at":"c"},"label":"5"},{"kind":"measure","from":{"at":"e"},"to":{"at":"f"},"label":"10"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"proportion","anchor":{"el":"scale","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\frac{DE}{AB}=\\frac{DF}{AC}=\\frac{EF}{BC}=2"},{"op":"text","id":"rule","anchor":{"el":"proportion","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"One scale factor for all matching sides"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If a corresponding small side were 7, how long would the large side be?","expected_gist":"14, because every corresponding side is doubled"}}}
